export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"bimcraft","addon":"plant","name":"Plant","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"plant-start","scope":"addon:plant","title":"Plant: getting started","summary":"What the Plant add-on does in BimCraft, how to switch it on, and what it leaves alone.","section":"start","order":1,"keywords":["plant","process","factory","pipe spec","isometric","equipment","nozzle","layout","piping","add-on"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-is","text":"What it is","level":2},{"id":"what-needs-a-newer-bimcraft","text":"What needs a newer BimCraft","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-is\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPlant is an add-on for BimCraft that comes with it and is free. It adds four things for process and factory work: \u003Cstrong\u003Epipe specs\u003C/strong\u003E checked against the pipe runs in the model, \u003Cstrong\u003Eisometric drawings\u003C/strong\u003E of pipe runs, \u003Cstrong\u003Eprocess equipment\u003C/strong\u003E with nozzles saved as an IFC file, and a \u003Cstrong\u003Elayout check\u003C/strong\u003E for aisles, the space beside each item and floor load.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is off until you switch it on: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlant\u003C/span\u003E. Its tools are in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlant\u003C/span\u003E tab of the right panel, in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E (a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPipe spec\u003C/span\u003E section when you select a pipe run) and in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA drafting aid, not an engineering approval.\u003C/strong\u003E The specs, the equipment shapes and the limits are generic and ours: the sizes and walls come from the public tables, the equipment is drawn in the right proportions but with no maker&#039;s dimensions, the aisle and floor-load limits are yours to set. The person responsible for the plant decides what is right.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-needs-a-newer-bimcraft\"\u003EWhat needs a newer BimCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPutting equipment into the open model (the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace\u003C/span\u003E button in the equipment table) and the router&#039;s list of ports need BimCraft&#039;s add-on API 1.1, which this build has; in a build without it the Plant tab says so, and the equipment goes out as its own IFC file, which BimCraft opens like any other.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe router can draw the route it found as a pipe run (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw this run\u003C/span\u003E). The isometric balloons the spool numbers BimCraft gave a steel run when it was cut into spools. The layout check reads the equipment table and also equipment placed in the model from the library.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe add-on changes the model only when you press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace\u003C/span\u003E (one undo step); everything else reads it.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What it is Plant is an add-on for BimCraft that comes with it and is free. It adds four things for process and factory work: pipe specs checked against the pipe runs in the model, isometric drawings of pipe runs, process equipment with nozzles saved as an IFC file, and a layout check for aisles, the space beside each item and floor load. It is off until you switch it on: Settings › General › Add-ons › Plant. Its tools are in the Plant tab of the right panel, in Properties (a Pipe spec section when you select a pipe run) and in Export. A drafting aid, not an engineering approval. The specs, the equipment shapes and the limits are generic and ours: the sizes and walls come from the public tables, the equipment is drawn in the right proportions but with no maker's dimensions, the aisle and floor-load limits are yours to set. The person responsible for the plant decides what is right. What needs a newer BimCraft Putting equipment into the open model (the Place button in the equipment table) and the router's list of ports need BimCraft's add-on API 1.1, which this build has; in a build without it the Plant tab says so, and the equipment goes out as its own IFC file, which BimCraft opens like any other. The router can draw the route it found as a pipe run (Draw this run). The isometric balloons the spool numbers BimCraft gave a steel run when it was cut into spools. The layout check reads the equipment table and also equipment placed in the model from the library. The add-on changes the model only when you press Place (one undo step); everything else reads it."},{"slug":"plant-specs","scope":"addon:plant","title":"Plant: pipe specs","summary":"What a pipe spec is, the five that come with the add-on, and how a pipe run is checked against its spec.","section":"tasks","order":10,"keywords":["pipe spec","specification","class","size","valve","branch","bend","elbow","steel","copper","pvc","pex"],"headings":[{"id":"what-a-spec-is","text":"What a spec is","level":2},{"id":"check-a-run","text":"Check a run","level":2},{"id":"branches-and-bends","text":"Branches and bends","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-a-spec-is\"\u003EWhat a spec is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA pipe spec is a class of pipe the way a plant writes it: the material and wall, the smallest and largest size, the valves it may carry and at which sizes, whether its elbows are long or short radius, and how a branch is made off a header of each size.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFive come with the add-on, written from the public size tables (steel to ASME B36.10M, copper to ASTM B88, PVC to ASTM D1785, PEX to ASTM F876): carbon steel Sch 40 (\u003Ccode\u003ECS40\u003C/code\u003E) and Sch 80 (\u003Ccode\u003ECS80\u003C/code\u003E), copper type L (\u003Ccode\u003ECU-L\u003C/code\u003E), PVC Sch 40 (\u003Ccode\u003EPVC40\u003C/code\u003E) and PEX (\u003Ccode\u003EPEX9\u003C/code\u003E). They are general-service starting points and ours; a plant&#039;s own spec book overrides them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA run is matched to its spec by the \u003Cstrong\u003Ematerial and class\u003C/strong\u003E it already carries, so nothing has to be added to a run to check it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"check-a-run\"\u003ECheck a run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect a pipe run: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E shows a \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPipe spec\u003C/span\u003E section — the spec, the sizes it allows, whether this run&#039;s size and each valve are allowed, the bends, and how branches of 4:1, 2:1 and 1:1 are made. Select several runs and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck the selected pipe runs\u003C/span\u003E in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlant\u003C/span\u003E tab to list every finding.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFinding\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELevel\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeans\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo pipe spec\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ewarning\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe run&#039;s material and class match no spec\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESize not allowed\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eerror\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe run&#039;s size is outside the spec&#039;s sizes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EValve not allowed\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eerror\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethe spec does not allow that kind of valve at that size\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"branches-and-bends\"\u003EBranches and bends\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA branch up to a quarter of its header&#039;s size is a stub-in on the steel specs; larger ones are reducing tees; equal sizes are tees. A branch larger than its header is not a branch. A long-radius elbow&#039;s centre line is 1.5 × the nominal size; a short one is one nominal size (at least the outside diameter).\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What a spec is A pipe spec is a class of pipe the way a plant writes it: the material and wall, the smallest and largest size, the valves it may carry and at which sizes, whether its elbows are long or short radius, and how a branch is made off a header of each size. Five come with the add-on, written from the public size tables (steel to ASME B36.10M, copper to ASTM B88, PVC to ASTM D1785, PEX to ASTM F876): carbon steel Sch 40 (CS40) and Sch 80 (CS80), copper type L (CU-L), PVC Sch 40 (PVC40) and PEX (PEX9). They are general-service starting points and ours; a plant's own spec book overrides them. A run is matched to its spec by the material and class it already carries, so nothing has to be added to a run to check it. Check a run Select a pipe run: Properties shows a Pipe spec section — the spec, the sizes it allows, whether this run's size and each valve are allowed, the bends, and how branches of 4:1, 2:1 and 1:1 are made. Select several runs and press Check the selected pipe runs in the Plant tab to list every finding. Finding Level Means No pipe spec warning the run's material and class match no spec Size not allowed error the run's size is outside the spec's sizes Valve not allowed error the spec does not allow that kind of valve at that size Branches and bends A branch up to a quarter of its header's size is a stub-in on the steel specs; larger ones are reducing tees; equal sizes are tees. A branch larger than its header is not a branch. A long-radius elbow's centre line is 1.5 × the nominal size; a short one is one nominal size (at least the outside diameter)."},{"slug":"plant-iso","scope":"addon:plant","title":"Plant: isometric drawings","summary":"Save the isometric of selected pipe runs as SVG or DXF, with dimensions, elevations, valves, welds, spool balloons and a bill of materials.","section":"tasks","order":20,"keywords":["isometric","iso","drawing","dimension","elevation","bill of materials","bom","spool","weld","svg","dxf"],"headings":[{"id":"make-one","text":"Make one","level":2},{"id":"what-is-on-it","text":"What is on it","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"make-one\"\u003EMake one\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESelect one or more pipe runs in the model, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cstrong\u003EIsometric of the selected pipe runs\u003C/strong\u003E (SVG or DXF), or the buttons in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlant\u003C/span\u003E tab. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBill of materials (CSV)\u003C/span\u003E saves the list on its own.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-on-it\"\u003EWhat is on it\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe pipe on its centre line in the usual 30° projection: east to the right and up, north to the left and up, up stays up. The north arrow shows the plant&#039;s north.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA dimension on every leg: its true length along the pipe, in millimetres, or feet and inches for a drawing in inches.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe elevation at each end and where the run changes level; the run&#039;s tag.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA weld dot at each elbow, valves as symbols turned along the pipe, a flow arrow.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESpool balloons with the numbers BimCraft gave a steel run when it was cut into spools (Pipes and ducts › Spools); nothing is numbered twice.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA bill of materials: pipe length by size and spec, elbows by angle, valves by kind, welds. A title block with the project, drawing number, revision, drawn by and date.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot to scale.\u003C/strong\u003E The drawing says so. It is fitted to an A3 sheet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe DXF opens in CadCraft and other CAD programs: lines, circles, solid fills and text on layers named \u003Ccode\u003EISO-PIPE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EISO-DIM\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EISO-SYMBOL\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EISO-TEXT\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EISO-BOM\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003EISO-FRAME\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Make one Select one or more pipe runs in the model, then Export › Isometric of the selected pipe runs (SVG or DXF), or the buttons in the Plant tab. Bill of materials (CSV) saves the list on its own. What is on it The pipe on its centre line in the usual 30° projection: east to the right and up, north to the left and up, up stays up. The north arrow shows the plant's north. A dimension on every leg: its true length along the pipe, in millimetres, or feet and inches for a drawing in inches. The elevation at each end and where the run changes level; the run's tag. A weld dot at each elbow, valves as symbols turned along the pipe, a flow arrow. Spool balloons with the numbers BimCraft gave a steel run when it was cut into spools (Pipes and ducts › Spools); nothing is numbered twice. A bill of materials: pipe length by size and spec, elbows by angle, valves by kind, welds. A title block with the project, drawing number, revision, drawn by and date. Not to scale. The drawing says so. It is fitted to an A3 sheet. The DXF opens in CadCraft and other CAD programs: lines, circles, solid fills and text on layers named ISO-PIPE, ISO-DIM, ISO-SYMBOL, ISO-TEXT, ISO-BOM and ISO-FRAME."},{"slug":"plant-equipment","scope":"addon:plant","title":"Plant: process equipment and layout","summary":"Add generic equipment with nozzles, save it as an IFC file with a port at every nozzle, and check the layout for aisles, access and floor load.","section":"tasks","order":30,"keywords":["equipment","vessel","tank","pump","exchanger","column","nozzle","port","ifc","layout","aisle","clearance","floor load"],"headings":[{"id":"equipment-with-nozzles","text":"Equipment with nozzles","level":2},{"id":"in-the-open-model","text":"In the open model","level":2},{"id":"route-a-pipe","text":"Route a pipe","level":2},{"id":"layout-check","text":"Layout check","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"equipment-with-nozzles\"\u003EEquipment with nozzles\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlant\u003C/span\u003E tab keeps a table of equipment in this browser: vertical and horizontal vessel, storage tank, column, centrifugal pump, shell and tube exchanger and plate exchanger. Choose a type, give it a tag, a place (X, Y, Z in millimetres), a turn about Z and, if you know it, a weight, and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach is drawn in closed solids of the right proportions and has \u003Cstrong\u003Enozzles\u003C/strong\u003E: where a pipe meets it, with a position, the direction the pipe leaves in, a size and a flow direction. They are generic shapes — no maker&#039;s dimensions — so use your vendor&#039;s figures where they matter.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as IFC file\u003C/span\u003E writes the table as an IFC4 file: each item as the right class (a pump, a tank, a heat exchanger), its solids, its tag, and one distribution port per nozzle with its flow direction and size, so the pipe runs of any program can connect to it. Open the file in BimCraft like any other.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-the-open-model\"\u003EIn the open model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace\u003C/span\u003E on a row (or the &quot;Place equipment in the model&quot; command, for every row not placed yet) writes the item into the open model through BimCraft&#039;s add-on API: the equipment, its tag and the property set \u003Ccode\u003ECadCraft_Plant\u003C/code\u003E, and a port at every nozzle, written the way BimCraft&#039;s own equipment ports are (\u003Ccode\u003ECadCraft_Port\u003C/code\u003E: size, nominal size, flow direction), so a pipe started or ended there snaps to it and Check connections sees it. It lands on the model&#039;s first storey at the place in the table; it is one undo step, and Undo takes the equipment and its ports out again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"route-a-pipe\"\u003ERoute a pipe\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERoute a pipe\u003C/span\u003E lists the ports of the model. Choose two, a spec and a size and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFind a route\u003C/span\u003E: the router leaves each nozzle straight out, runs only along X, Y and Z, keeps clear of the equipment you placed, never bends twice within two pipe diameters (150 mm at least) and prefers few bends and few climbs. The result is its length, its bends and its corners, in the storey&#039;s own millimetres. Press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDraw this run\u003C/span\u003E to put it into the model as a pipe run, in the spec&#039;s material and class and in the pipe system you choose (BimCraft has no process system of its own: chilled water, heating water, cold water, hot water, gas or fire protection). It is one undo step, and its two ends are joined to the nozzles they touch. Both ports must be on the same level.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"layout-check\"\u003ELayout check\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck the layout\u003C/span\u003E looks at the table, and at equipment placed in the model from the library (its width, depth and weight), for: items that \u003Cstrong\u003Eoverlap\u003C/strong\u003E; \u003Cstrong\u003Eaisles\u003C/strong\u003E narrower than your limit (900 mm to start with); the \u003Cstrong\u003Espace beside each item\u003C/strong\u003E that must stay free (800 mm in front, 300 mm elsewhere to start with) when something stands in it; and \u003Cstrong\u003Efloor load\u003C/strong\u003E over your limit (1000 kg/m² to start with). The limits are numbers you set; they are not a code requirement, and the check is a drafting aid.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Equipment with nozzles The Plant tab keeps a table of equipment in this browser: vertical and horizontal vessel, storage tank, column, centrifugal pump, shell and tube exchanger and plate exchanger. Choose a type, give it a tag, a place (X, Y, Z in millimetres), a turn about Z and, if you know it, a weight, and press Add. Each is drawn in closed solids of the right proportions and has nozzles: where a pipe meets it, with a position, the direction the pipe leaves in, a size and a flow direction. They are generic shapes — no maker's dimensions — so use your vendor's figures where they matter. Save as IFC file writes the table as an IFC4 file: each item as the right class (a pump, a tank, a heat exchanger), its solids, its tag, and one distribution port per nozzle with its flow direction and size, so the pipe runs of any program can connect to it. Open the file in BimCraft like any other. In the open model Place on a row (or the \"Place equipment in the model\" command, for every row not placed yet) writes the item into the open model through BimCraft's add-on API: the equipment, its tag and the property set CadCraft_Plant, and a port at every nozzle, written the way BimCraft's own equipment ports are (CadCraft_Port: size, nominal size, flow direction), so a pipe started or ended there snaps to it and Check connections sees it. It lands on the model's first storey at the place in the table; it is one undo step, and Undo takes the equipment and its ports out again. Route a pipe Route a pipe lists the ports of the model. Choose two, a spec and a size and press Find a route: the router leaves each nozzle straight out, runs only along X, Y and Z, keeps clear of the equipment you placed, never bends twice within two pipe diameters (150 mm at least) and prefers few bends and few climbs. The result is its length, its bends and its corners, in the storey's own millimetres. Press Draw this run to put it into the model as a pipe run, in the spec's material and class and in the pipe system you choose (BimCraft has no process system of its own: chilled water, heating water, cold water, hot water, gas or fire protection). It is one undo step, and its two ends are joined to the nozzles they touch. Both ports must be on the same level. Layout check Check the layout looks at the table, and at equipment placed in the model from the library (its width, depth and weight), for: items that overlap; aisles narrower than your limit (900 mm to start with); the space beside each item that must stay free (800 mm in front, 300 mm elsewhere to start with) when something stands in it; and floor load over your limit (1000 kg/m² to start with). The limits are numbers you set; they are not a code requirement, and the check is a drafting aid."},{"slug":"plant-process","scope":"addon:plant","title":"Plant: check the pipe runs against a process model","summary":"Load a process model, tie the pipe runs and equipment to its lines and units, and see each line's velocity and pressure drop from the run as drawn, and what the P&ID has to change.","section":"tasks","order":40,"keywords":["process","flowsheet","hydraulics","velocity","pressure drop","line size","P&ID","process.json","stream"],"headings":[],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA drafting and study aid, not an engineering approval. No representation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen the \u003Cstrong\u003EPlant\u003C/strong\u003E tab and the \u003Cstrong\u003EProcess model\u003C/strong\u003E section. Load a \u003Ccode\u003Eprocess.json\u003C/code\u003E (made in CadCraft&#039;s P&amp;ID, SuperCad Studio or by the assistant), then press \u003Cstrong\u003ECheck the runs\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPipe runs are tied to the model&#039;s lines by their system name (the line number), and equipment by its tag.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEach line&#039;s velocity and pressure drop come from the run as you drew it: its length, the elbows at its corners, its valves, the height it rises and its size. A bigger pipe lowers both.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe list says, for each line, the pipe size, velocity and drop; and anything that disagrees (a different size on the P&amp;ID, equipment missing, a velocity over the limit) appears in the Problems panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe command \u003Cstrong\u003ECheck against the process model\u003C/strong\u003E does the same from the command list, and \u003Cstrong\u003EProcess model (JSON)\u003C/strong\u003E in Export writes the model back, with the links it made.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EViscosity is an assumed default until the line gives one; the result says so.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A drafting and study aid, not an engineering approval. No representation. Open the Plant tab and the Process model section. Load a process.json (made in CadCraft's P&ID, SuperCad Studio or by the assistant), then press Check the runs. Pipe runs are tied to the model's lines by their system name (the line number), and equipment by its tag. Each line's velocity and pressure drop come from the run as you drew it: its length, the elbows at its corners, its valves, the height it rises and its size. A bigger pipe lowers both. The list says, for each line, the pipe size, velocity and drop; and anything that disagrees (a different size on the P&ID, equipment missing, a velocity over the limit) appears in the Problems panel. The command Check against the process model does the same from the command list, and Process model (JSON) in Export writes the model back, with the links it made. Viscosity is an assumed default until the line gives one; the result says so."},{"slug":"plant-engineering","scope":"addon:plant","title":"Engineering checks in the Plant panel","summary":"Vessel, pipe stress, relief, consequence zones and hazardous areas in the Plant panel, and what each does with the model.","section":"tasks","order":60,"keywords":["pressure vessel","pipe stress","relief valve","consequence","hazardous area","zones","equipment table"],"headings":[],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA calculation aid, not an engineering approval. These five calculators are not engineer-reviewed: every result says so, and one engineer will review them together before any number is used for design.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen the \u003Cstrong\u003EEngineering checks\u003C/strong\u003E section of the Plant tab. Pick a calculator, change the sample input and press \u003Cstrong\u003ERun\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EVessel.\u003C/strong\u003E The shell, head, flat head, nozzle reinforcement with its limits, and a drawing of the vessel. \u003Cstrong\u003EAdd the vessel to the equipment table\u003C/strong\u003E puts a vertical or horizontal vessel of the size you worked out into the Plant table.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPipe stress.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Cstrong\u003EUse a pipe run of the model\u003C/strong\u003E fills the input from the run with the most points: its points and size, and an anchor at each end. Set the temperature, the supports and, for more realistic results, \u003Ccode\u003Eflexibility\u003C/code\u003E (bends are less stiff than straight pipe) and \u003Ccode\u003Emu\u003C/code\u003E (friction on the rests).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERelief.\u003C/strong\u003E Gas, steam, liquid and fire areas with the orifice letter, a rupture disc in series, the outlet line and the thrust of an open discharge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EConsequence\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003Earea.\u003C/strong\u003E Radiation, blast and plume zones, or hazardous-area zones from release sources. \u003Cstrong\u003EPut the zones in the model\u003C/strong\u003E makes each zone a closed solid in the open model, in one undo step.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe numbers are only as good as the input and the typed constants; the Problems list says which were not confirmed against the standards.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMore calculators (round 4).\u003C/strong\u003E The same section also runs: \u003Cstrong\u003Ebell\u003C/strong\u003E (Bell-Delaware geometry of a shell-and-tube exchanger, and the rating when you give your own coefficient pack), \u003Cstrong\u003Evessel2\u003C/strong\u003E (external pressure and the stiffening ring from the chart pairs you type, the vessel body outline), \u003Cstrong\u003Epumptrip\u003C/strong\u003E (a pump trip with check valve and run-down, with a trend), \u003Cstrong\u003Epipestress2\u003C/strong\u003E (pressure thrust, spring hanger choice from a maker&#039;s table, stop gaps), \u003Cstrong\u003Erelief2\u003C/strong\u003E (depressuring, control-valve failure, the load of each relief header), \u003Cstrong\u003Eblast\u003C/strong\u003E (multi-energy blast from your own curve pack), \u003Cstrong\u003Eheavygas\u003C/strong\u003E (a heavy-gas cloud with your own constants) and \u003Cstrong\u003Epacks\u003C/strong\u003E (a table with a clause per row becomes a threshold, reactivity or area-classification pack). A calculator that needs a table from a book says which book and stops; nothing is typed in for you.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFitting losses (draft table).\u003C/strong\u003E The \u003Cem\u003EFitting losses\u003C/em\u003E calculator picks a fitting or valve from the 2-K and 3-K tables and gives K and the head loss for a Reynolds number, pipe size and velocity. The table is a draft: its numbers come from the tables of the MIT-licensed \u003Ccode\u003Efluids\u003C/code\u003E library for the Hooper (1981) and Darby (1999) methods and have not been checked against the papers by an engineer. Put the K on a pipe link to use it in a network.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENetwork fittings, compressor and pump.\u003C/strong\u003E The Pipe network calculator takes a \u003Ccode\u003Efit\u003C/code\u003E list on a link (fittings from the draft table, \u003Ccode\u003E&quot;fittingPack&quot;: &quot;draft&quot;\u003C/code\u003E): the K is worked out at each step from the link&#039;s own Reynolds number and diameter and added to the typed K. The Compressor calculator gives head, shaft work, power and outlet temperature for an ideal gas with the k and Z you give (it states its assumptions); the Pump calculator gives the head from its curve and the margin of NPSH available over required.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A calculation aid, not an engineering approval. These five calculators are not engineer-reviewed: every result says so, and one engineer will review them together before any number is used for design. Open the Engineering checks section of the Plant tab. Pick a calculator, change the sample input and press Run. Vessel. The shell, head, flat head, nozzle reinforcement with its limits, and a drawing of the vessel. Add the vessel to the equipment table puts a vertical or horizontal vessel of the size you worked out into the Plant table. Pipe stress. Use a pipe run of the model fills the input from the run with the most points: its points and size, and an anchor at each end. Set the temperature, the supports and, for more realistic results, flexibility (bends are less stiff than straight pipe) and mu (friction on the rests). Relief. Gas, steam, liquid and fire areas with the orifice letter, a rupture disc in series, the outlet line and the thrust of an open discharge. Consequence and area. Radiation, blast and plume zones, or hazardous-area zones from release sources. Put the zones in the model makes each zone a closed solid in the open model, in one undo step. The numbers are only as good as the input and the typed constants; the Problems list says which were not confirmed against the standards. More calculators (round 4). The same section also runs: bell (Bell-Delaware geometry of a shell-and-tube exchanger, and the rating when you give your own coefficient pack), vessel2 (external pressure and the stiffening ring from the chart pairs you type, the vessel body outline), pumptrip (a pump trip with check valve and run-down, with a trend), pipestress2 (pressure thrust, spring hanger choice from a maker's table, stop gaps), relief2 (depressuring, control-valve failure, the load of each relief header), blast (multi-energy blast from your own curve pack), heavygas (a heavy-gas cloud with your own constants) and packs (a table with a clause per row becomes a threshold, reactivity or area-classification pack). A calculator that needs a table from a book says which book and stops; nothing is typed in for you. Fitting losses (draft table). The Fitting losses calculator picks a fitting or valve from the 2-K and 3-K tables and gives K and the head loss for a Reynolds number, pipe size and velocity. The table is a draft: its numbers come from the tables of the MIT-licensed fluids library for the Hooper (1981) and Darby (1999) methods and have not been checked against the papers by an engineer. Put the K on a pipe link to use it in a network. Network fittings, compressor and pump. The Pipe network calculator takes a fit list on a link (fittings from the draft table, \"fittingPack\": \"draft\"): the K is worked out at each step from the link's own Reynolds number and diameter and added to the typed K. The Compressor calculator gives head, shaft work, power and outlet temperature for an ideal gas with the k and Z you give (it states its assumptions); the Pump calculator gives the head from its curve and the margin of NPSH available over required."},{"slug":"plant-p4","scope":"addon:plant","title":"Plant: place the plant with its pipes, batch schedule in 4D, live values","summary":"Put the process model's layout in the model with its pipes, show a batch recipe in the 4D panel, and tint equipment by plant readings.","section":"tasks","order":65,"keywords":["process layout","place","nozzle","pipes","batch","recipe","4d","schedule","live","readings","historian","tint"],"headings":[{"id":"the-plant-and-its-pipes","text":"The plant and its pipes","level":2},{"id":"a-batch-recipe-in-4d","text":"A batch recipe in 4D","level":2},{"id":"live-values","text":"Live values","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA drafting and study aid, not an engineering approval. No representation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen the \u003Cstrong\u003EPlant\u003C/strong\u003E tab and the section \u003Cstrong\u003EPlant with pipes, batch schedule, live values\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-plant-and-its-pipes\"\u003EThe plant and its pipes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith a process model that has a layout loaded (Process model section), pick a pipe system and press \u003Cstrong\u003EPlace the plant and its pipes\u003C/strong\u003E. The equipment goes into the equipment table and into the open model, then every run of the layout is routed from the first free outlet nozzle of one item to the first free inlet nozzle of the next, round the equipment, and drawn as a pipe run. One undo step each. A run is left out, and the panel says why, when an end is not in the model, has no free nozzle, or the two ends are on different storeys, or no route was found.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-batch-recipe-in-4d\"\u003EA batch recipe in 4D\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe recipe is JSON (steps with a duration and the class of equipment, and the equipment with its size). \u003Cstrong\u003EShow the batch schedule in 4D\u003C/strong\u003E sends it to the 4D tab as tasks: one per step and batch, linked finish-to-start, each on its equipment, found in the model by its Tag. Open the 4D tab first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"live-values\"\u003ELive values\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPaste readings as CSV with the header \u003Ccode\u003Etag,time,value,quality\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Cstrong\u003EShow the values\u003C/strong\u003E: the equipment whose Tag matches is tinted in the 3D view by its state (green ok, red high or bad, blue low, grey stale). \u003Cstrong\u003EClear the tint\u003C/strong\u003E takes it off. Read only: nothing here controls equipment, and nothing here is an alarm system.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A drafting and study aid, not an engineering approval. No representation. Open the Plant tab and the section Plant with pipes, batch schedule, live values. The plant and its pipes With a process model that has a layout loaded (Process model section), pick a pipe system and press Place the plant and its pipes. The equipment goes into the equipment table and into the open model, then every run of the layout is routed from the first free outlet nozzle of one item to the first free inlet nozzle of the next, round the equipment, and drawn as a pipe run. One undo step each. A run is left out, and the panel says why, when an end is not in the model, has no free nozzle, or the two ends are on different storeys, or no route was found. A batch recipe in 4D The recipe is JSON (steps with a duration and the class of equipment, and the equipment with its size). Show the batch schedule in 4D sends it to the 4D tab as tasks: one per step and batch, linked finish-to-start, each on its equipment, found in the model by its Tag. Open the 4D tab first. Live values Paste readings as CSV with the header tag,time,value,quality and press Show the values: the equipment whose Tag matches is tinted in the 3D view by its state (green ok, red high or bad, blue low, grey stale). Clear the tint takes it off. Read only: nothing here controls equipment, and nothing here is an alarm system."},{"slug":"plant-connect","scope":"addon:plant","title":"Plant: connect to a device and the tag map","summary":"List the plant devices, type the tag map, try it with a CSV of readings, and see equipment tinted by Tag. Read only.","section":"tasks","order":66,"keywords":["live","device","modbus","mqtt","bacnet","opc ua","tag map","readings","tint","studio"],"headings":[],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ERead only: nothing here writes to a device, raises an alarm or acts as a safety function.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen the \u003Cstrong\u003EPlant\u003C/strong\u003E tab and the section \u003Cstrong\u003EConnect to a device\u003C/strong\u003E. It is the same panel as in the P&amp;ID add-on: add devices, type the tag map, press \u003Cstrong\u003ECheck the map\u003C/strong\u003E, then \u003Cstrong\u003EShow these readings\u003C/strong\u003E with a CSV of \u003Ccode\u003Etag,time,value,quality\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach reading tints the element of the equipment table that has that \u003Cstrong\u003ETag\u003C/strong\u003E: normal readings one colour, high, low and bad quality others, with the value as its label. \u003Cstrong\u003ERead the devices\u003C/strong\u003E needs CadCraft Studio; in a browser tab use the CSV.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Read only: nothing here writes to a device, raises an alarm or acts as a safety function. Open the Plant tab and the section Connect to a device. It is the same panel as in the P&ID add-on: add devices, type the tag map, press Check the map, then Show these readings with a CSV of tag,time,value,quality. Each reading tints the element of the equipment table that has that Tag: normal readings one colour, high, low and bad quality others, with the value as its label. Read the devices needs CadCraft Studio; in a browser tab use the CSV."},{"slug":"plant-optimise","scope":"addon:plant","title":"Plant: optimise overnight, on other computers too","summary":"Try many settings of the process model, rank them by energy, cost or emissions, and let the other computers on your network solve the variants.","section":"tasks","order":67,"keywords":["optimise","optimize","overnight","study","variants","process","lan","nodes","studio","energy","trade-off"],"headings":[],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA study aid, not an engineering approval. No representation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen the \u003Cstrong\u003EPlant\u003C/strong\u003E tab and the section \u003Cstrong\u003EOptimise overnight\u003C/strong\u003E. It needs a process model (bring one in with the process file first).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EEdit the study. Each \u003Cstrong\u003Evariable\u003C/strong\u003E names a unit of the model, one of its parameters, a range and the number of steps. Each \u003Cstrong\u003Eobjective\u003C/strong\u003E names a metric (\u003Ccode\u003Eheat_kW\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ecool_kW\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eshaft_kW\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eenergy_kW\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eoperating_per_h\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eco2_kg_h\u003C/code\u003E or a stream property) and whether to minimise or maximise it. Cost and emissions need the prices and factors you type under \u003Ccode\u003Eprices\u003C/code\u003E and \u003Ccode\u003Efactors\u003C/code\u003E; nothing is assumed.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cstrong\u003ERun the study\u003C/strong\u003E. The panel says how many variants were solved and ranks the best ones.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOther computers.\u003C/strong\u003E In CadCraft Studio, a computer that turned on \u003Cem\u003ELet others render on this computer\u003C/em\u003E (Settings, Rendering) also solves process variants for you once you connect to it with its code. The panel says how many are connected. A variant a computer cannot take, or a computer that leaves in the middle of a run, is solved here, so no variant is lost. Outside Studio every variant is solved on this computer.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A study aid, not an engineering approval. No representation. Open the Plant tab and the section Optimise overnight. It needs a process model (bring one in with the process file first). Edit the study. Each variable names a unit of the model, one of its parameters, a range and the number of steps. Each objective names a metric (heat_kW, cool_kW, shaft_kW, energy_kW, operating_per_h, co2_kg_h or a stream property) and whether to minimise or maximise it. Cost and emissions need the prices and factors you type under prices and factors; nothing is assumed. Press Run the study. The panel says how many variants were solved and ranks the best ones. Other computers. In CadCraft Studio, a computer that turned on Let others render on this computer (Settings, Rendering) also solves process variants for you once you connect to it with its code. The panel says how many are connected. A variant a computer cannot take, or a computer that leaves in the middle of a run, is solved here, so no variant is lost. Outside Studio every variant is solved on this computer."}]};
